CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: Paul says who Christ is in several passages, each in its own terms and against its own problem, and read together they present one person who is God and who became man, with the manner left unsaid. It has tried to show that from the text. In Philippians 2 he who was in the form of God took the form of a servant. In Colossians he is the image of the invisible God, the Creator, and the one in whom all the fulness of the Godhead dwells bodily. In 1 Timothy God was manifest in the flesh. In Titus he is the great God and our Saviour. In Romans he is over all, God blessed for ever. And in the same letters he is the man Christ Jesus, made of a woman and of the seed of David."),
("p", "The passages share a subject and a few words, and they differ in the problem each answers. The volume has asked of each the same questions, and has not carried the answer of one passage into another. It has also said where each passage stops. None of them says how the form of God and the form of a servant are one person. None uses the words of the later creeds. The volume has not supplied them."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What is the example set before Philippi?", "One who was in the form of God took the form of a servant and humbled himself", "Phil 2:5–8"),
    ("What is said of his state before the flesh?", "In the form of God; equal with God; the image of the invisible God", "Phil 2:6; Col 1:15"),
    ("What is firstborn of every creature?", "A title of rank, given with the reason that by him all things were created", "Col 1:15, 16"),
    ("What is said of creation?", "By him and for him all things were created; he is before all things", "Col 1:16, 17; 1Cor 8:6"),
    ("What is said of the fulness?", "All the fulness of the Godhead dwells in him bodily", "Col 1:19; 2:9"),
    ("What is said of God and the flesh?", "God was manifest in the flesh", "1Tim 3:16"),
    ("Who is the Saviour in Titus 2:13?", "The great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ, best read as one person", "Titus 2:13"),
    ("What is said at Romans 9:5?", "As concerning the flesh Christ came; he is over all, God blessed for ever, read as the KJV has it", "Rom 9:5"),
    ("Is he a man?", "Yes: the man Christ Jesus, made of a woman and of the seed of David", "1Tim 2:5; Gal 4:4; Rom 1:3"),
    ("Do the passages say how the two are one?", "No; each leaves the manner unsaid", "Chapters 14 to 16"),
  ], [0.30, 0.46, 0.24]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says. In either case the reader will have read the passages in their places."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. Constable, Piper and Wright each see, in the passages they comment on, that Paul gives Christ a place beside God, and each is right to see it. Where one of them adds a method, an inference or a sonship the sentences do not state, the volume has said so and has answered from the text. If the volume has been unfair, the verses will show it."),
]

